Potential # Closed non-displaced distal phalanx fracture from crush injury (door slam) to finger
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Severe throbbing fingertip pain, Distal phalanx pulp swelling, Door crush mechanism with subungual hematoma

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-044
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe throbbing finger pain following a crush injury, requiring urgent assessment for an open tuft fracture and subungual hematoma decompression.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute door crush injury causing extreme, unremitting throbbing pain and tense fingertip swelling. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to assess for an open, contaminated tuft fracture requiring immediate antibiotics, and to safely decompress a tense subungual hematoma to salvage the nail bed before daytime clinics opened.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I slammed my finger in the heavy door; it is throbbing uncontrollably and the tip is swollen tight.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a history of a direct heavy crush mechanism (finger trapped in a closing door) 1 hour ago. This resulted in severe, throbbing localized pain at the tip of the affected digit immediately post-injury. The patient reports significant tense swelling and erythema confined exclusively to the distal phalanx pulp. There is extreme tenderness to deep palpation across the volar tuft and dorsal nail bed. The patient notes an associated tense subungual hematoma forming under an intact nail plate.
Functional Status: Visibly guarding the hand; unable to use the injured digit due to extreme pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: None; mechanism was accidental.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Alert, in severe acute localized distress, elevating the hand to head level.
Physical Exam: Digit examination reveals severe throbbing fingertip pain with tense distal phalanx pulp swelling. A subungual hematoma covering approximately 40% of the nail bed is present beneath a structurally intact nail plate. There is no visible angular or rotational clinical deformity of the fingertip, nor is there a jagged laceration connecting the skin to the bone (closed injury). Preserved active and passive range of motion at the distal interphalangeal joint confirms intact deep flexor and extensor tendon function. Proximal capillary refill is normal, though distal tip assessment is obscured by swelling.
Environmental Observations: Heavy internal door identified as the cause.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed non-displaced distal phalanx fracture - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Open tuft fracture - Ruled out due to: Completely intact skin and nail plate, preventing bacterial ingress into the fracture site.

Extensor tendon avulsion (Mallet finger) - Ruled out due to: Intact and strong active extension of the distal interphalangeal joint against gravity.

Flexor digitorum profundus avulsion (Jersey finger) - Ruled out due to: Preserved active flexion of the distal interphalangeal joint.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

A high-force door crush injury to the fingertip carries a very high probability of an underlying distal tuft fracture. Bedside clinical assessment confidently excluded an open fracture (which would mandate prophylactic antibiotics) and confirmed functionally intact tendons. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically required to safely perform bedside nail trephination for the tense subungual hematoma, instantly resolving the unbearable neuropathic compartment pain and preventing permanent germinal matrix ischemia, safely avoiding a painful emergency department presentation overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Performed bedside sterile nail trephination using electrocautery/large-bore needle, yielding immediate dark blood egress and profound, instant pain relief. Applied a simple non-adherent dressing. Advised standard anteroposterior, lateral, and oblique radiographs of the digit tomorrow to definitively confirm the likely non-displaced tuft fracture.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the finger constantly elevated above the heart and wrapped in the dressing. Trephination created a drainage hole; elevation uses gravity to pull the throbbing arterial pressure away from the tight fingertip space, preventing the unbearable pain from returning overnight.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular finger bandage/splint for protection.
Follow-up: Routine GP review tomorrow to review the digital x-ray and ensure the nail plate remains stable.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department or local GP if the finger becomes intensely red, hot, swollen beyond the tip, or if thick yellow/green pus begins oozing from the small drainage hole.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Distal tuft fractures are almost universally managed conservatively (exactly as done today) unless severely displaced; the nail splints the bone.
Clinical Course: The acute, agonizing throbbing has been relieved. The bone will heal over 4 to 6 weeks. The nail may eventually fall off painlessly in a few weeks as the new nail pushes it out.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if the severe throbbing returns and cannot be controlled, or if red streaks begin moving down your finger towards the hand.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The heavy door crushed the soft tissue against the bone, causing bleeding. Burning a tiny hole in the dead nail instantly releases that trapped, pressurized blood, which is what causes the extreme pain.
Actionable Steps: Get the finger x-ray tomorrow. Expect a small amount of dark blood to ooze from the hole for 24 hours. Do not submerge the finger in dirty water (like dishwater). Do not try to pull the nail off.

